  "query_preview": "..\n\nA cooling trend is on schedule to bring temperatures back down to a \nmore typical range for early November during the late week. The \nchange is brought by low pressure and associated cold fronts \nspanning the area from Quebec, across the Great Lakes, and into the \nMidwest. Smaller scale circulations within the long wave mid/upper \nlevel trough are also regulating greater coverage of showers toward \nthe Tri Cities and northern Thumb this afternoon. The pattern of \nshowers then shifts southward tonight along and ahead of the cold \nfront as it sweeps through SE Mi. Low temperatures are projected to \ndrop down into the mid 30s north, but after rain ends late tonight. \nLows hold up around 40 south of I-69 and across metro Detroit to \nensure no precipitation type concerns, although freezing level drops \ndown close to 1000 ft in model soundings through Friday morning. \n\nAn impressive round of mid to upper level height falls is shown in \nsatellite imagery this afternoon and model data tonight diving from \nnorthern Ontario into the central Great Lakes. This is the primary \ndriver of the frontal passage tonight before the system closes off \noverhead during Friday. Scattered to numerous coverage of showers is \nmaintained by the strong 500 mb cyclonic circulation and cold core, \nespecially during the afternoon as surface temperatures strain \ntoward 50, and as the low moves slowly across southern Lower Mi. \nTemperatures at 500 mb hover around -22 C, about normal for this \ntime of year, but with the added boost from strong forcing and \ndaytime instability. Shower coverage and intensity then pick up \nconsiderably Friday night as the low shifts eastward and the mid \nlevel trowal/deformation wraps in from the northern Great Lakes. \nIncreased model QPF reflects the 850-700 mb theta-e advection, \nstrong dynamics, and also a boost of enhancement from Lake Huron \nbefore the moisture axis slides eastward Saturday morning. \n\nThe 05/12Z models show a faster than expected eastward progression \nof the low pressure system considering its closed/amplified \ncharacter. The Friday Great Lakes system reaches upstate NY during \nSaturday leaving Lower Mi dry with temperatures rising back a few \ndegrees above normal by Sunday. Midwest surface high pressure builds \ninto the Great Lakes as the northern stream westerlies shift back \nnorth into Canada. The resulting farther north low pressure track \ntakes the edge off the next cold front expected during the Sunday \nnight/Monday timeframe. Guidance forecast temperatures around 60 \nlook reasonable given this larger scale pattern holds through the \nmiddle of next week.",
